 OPE: 4.1\; 4.10\; 4.17\; 3.1.4\; 8.1–8.4\; 8.16\; 9.1–9.2\nCAREER/EM
 BEDDED PURPOSE: Series/parallel networks\, voltage/current division\, me
 ter range/connection\, LTspice prediction vs measurement.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S
  RESOURCES:\n- Electronic Circuits playlist: https://www.youtube.com/pla
 ylist?list=PL0o_zxa4K1BV9E-N8tSExU1djL6slnjbL\n- ETA Common Formulas: ht
 tps://www.etai.org/Common_Formulas.pdf\n- LTspice: https://www.analog.co
 m/en/resources/design-tools-and-calculators/ltspice-simulator.html\n\nMA
 STERY GATE: Solve unseen series/parallel/KVL/KCLproblems\, predict test 
 points\, then verify a built divider network with DMM and explain any me
 asurement error.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: watching alone never counts. Use learn
  -> retrieve -> calculate/code ->build/measure -> debug -> document. Sta
 rt each non-exam study block with~10 minutes of spaced recall. Never use
  a missed session as justification for an unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\
 nTODAY — ACTION CHECKLIST:\n1. Open LAB-002 — Series / Parallel Netw
 ork Verification in Practice → Lab Center → Week 02.\n2. Choose one 
 route. Virtual needs: Web browser only. Physical needs: DMM\, breadboard
 \, resistor assortment\, low-voltage supply.\n3. Follow every numbered s
 tep under the route you selected\; predict the result before simulating\
 , building\, or measuring.\n4. Save the required evidence: Schematic + c
 alculations + predicted-vs-simulated table + troubleshooting path.\n5. M
 ark only the route you actually completed. A virtual completion does not
  claim physical bench experience.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP THESE LINKS:
 \n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your scanned/Goo
 gle Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\n• ETA
  6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nOFFICIA
 L STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Ch.3 p.19 series/paral
 lel formulas + Ch.4 pp.27–34.\nQUIZ / REVIEW: Ch.4 quiz p.34\, then Un
 it 2 review p.35.\nWHY: The book's DC-circuit sequence aligns directly w
 ith series/parallel\, KVL/KCL\, voltage dividers and troubleshooting.\nS
 OURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is the primary CETa textbook. Current E
 TA competency wording + current ETA errata control whenever the older bo
 ok differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNIN
 G OUTCOMES (WEEK 02)\nBy the end of this event\, you should be able to e
 xplain or demonstrate:\n• Analyze series and parallel resistor network
 s.\n• Use Kirchhoff's Voltage Law and Kirchhoff's Current Law to reaso
 n about circuit behavior.\n• Design and analyze a voltage divider\, in
 cluding the effect of loading.\n• Predict node voltages\, branch curre
 nts\, equivalent resistance\, and power.\n• Calculate equivalent resis
 tance\, node/branch values\, divider outputs\, and power.\n• Use KVL/K
 CL to check whether calculated results are internally consistent.\n• U
 se a DMM to verify a DC network and explain differences between predicte
 d and measured values.\n• Troubleshoot opens\, shorts\, wrong resistor
  values\, and wiring mistakes systematically.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe ass
 igned resources and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this
  outcome list. If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these i
 tems after the session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the c
 oncept on a short catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemen
 tal resource before closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE\nThis prac
 tical is the cumulative proof point for the week. The week is Green only
  when the knowledge can be used on real hardware/code/measurements witho
 ut following a step-by-step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish 
 the assigned work\, then check the learning outcomes below. If you canno
 t explain or demonstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on a short 
 catch-up list and repair it during the next available review block. No s
 preadsheet logging is required.
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
