 t-order reactive behavior.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is the pri
 mary CETa textbook. Current ETA competency wording + current ETA errata 
 control whenever the older book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFT
 ER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 04)\nBy the end of this ev
 ent\, you should be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Describe sine w
 aves and basic AC quantities: amplitude\, peak\, peak-to-peak\, frequenc
 y\, period\, phase\, and RMS.\n• Convert between frequency and period 
 and interpret waveform measurements.\n• Explain how capacitors and ind
 uctors store energy and oppose changes in voltage or current.\n• Descr
 ibe RC and RL transient behavior and the idea of a time constant.\n• C
 alculate frequency/period\, RMS or peak relationships where assigned\, a
 nd basic RC timing quantities.\n• Read waveform scales accurately and 
 connect numerical results to scope displays.\n• Recognize basic low-pa
 ss and high-pass behavior.\n• Use an oscilloscope to connect waveform 
 theory to measured circuit behavior.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned res
 ources and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this outcome 
 list. If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these items afte
 r the session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the concept on
  a short catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemental resou
 rce before closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE\nThis practical is 
