 g-amplifier and d B foundation\; labs add the practical embedded signal-
 conditioning angle.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book is the primary CE
 Ta textbook. Current ETA competency wording + current ETA errata control
  whenever the older book differs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODA
 Y — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 10)\nBy the end of this event\, y
 ou should be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Explain voltage gain\,
  current gain\, power gain\, loading\, input/output impedance\, and feed
 back.\n• Use decibels at a basic technician level and relate d B chang
 es to gain/loss.\n• Distinguish common amplifier classes and basic tra
 nsistor/FET amplifier roles.\n• Explain ideal op-amp assumptions at an
  introductory level.\n• Calculate basic voltage/power gain and d B rel
 ationships.\n• Predict amplifier output limits and identify clipping/l
 oading from numerical or waveform evidence.\n• Recognize inverting\, n
 on-inverting\, buffer/comparator-style applications and the role of feed
 back.\n• Measure or simulate an amplifier and diagnose clipping\, bias
 \, loading\, or gain problems.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resources
  and tasks above are intended to cover every item in this outcome list. 
 If you still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these items after the 
 session\, do not count that item as complete. Write the concept on a sho
 rt catch-up list and use the linked official or supplemental resource be
 fore closing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE\nThis practical is the cu
 mulative proof point for the week. The week is Green only when the knowl
 edge can be used on real hardware/code/measurements without following a 
 step-by-step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned wo
 rk\, then check the learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or de
 monstrate an outcome\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list a
 nd repair it during the next available review block. No spreadsheet logg
 ing is required.
