SUMMARY:W14 • Learn — C Fundamentals + Computer Architecture
DESCRIPTION:ALFRED UNIVERSITY | AU-ESET 301\n\nALFRED STUDY PATH — OPEN
  THESE WHEN YOU NEED THEM\n• Week module: https://numberclaw2.github.i
 o/alfred-university/week.html?week=14\n• Search this topic: https://nu
 mberclaw2.github.io/alfred-university/search.html?q=C%20Programming%20I%
 20%2B%20Computer%20Architecture\n• Engineering Library: https://number
 claw2.github.io/alfred-university/resources.html\nCAREER CONNECTION: C +
  MCU bring-up + serial buses + debug instrumentation.\nRESOURCE PRIORITY
 : CETa Core → Career Core → Deep Dive. Complete the required work fi
 rst\; use Deep Dive for repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK 14 FOCUS: C Progr
 amming I + Computer Architecture\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 15.1–15.4\; 15.6–1
 5.9\; 3.8\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: Compiler/linker\, types\, control fl
 ow\, functions\, arrays\, structs\, memory blocks\, CPU/buses/peripheral
 s.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES:\n- Beej’s Guide to C: https://beej.us/gui
 de/bgc/\n- Crash Course Computer Science: https://www.youtube.com/playli
 st?list=PL8dPuuaLjXtNlUrzyH5r6jN9ulIgZBpdo\n\nMASTERY GATE: Write and ex
 plain a small C program from scratch\; explain CPU/RAM/flash/peripherals
 /bus blocks and how software reaches hardware.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: Watching
  alone never counts. Use learn ->retrieve -> calculate/code -> build/mea
 sure -> debug -> document. start each non-exam study block with ~10 minu
 tes of spaced recall. Never use a missed session as justification for an
  unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY:\nStudy only the resources tied to
  this week’s competencies. Create a one-page summary and 5–10 recall
  prompts. first 10 min: spaced retrieval from older material.\n\nREFEREN
 CE HUB — KEEP THESE LINKS:\n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6
 th ed.: use your scanned/Google Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers 
 in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\n• ETA 6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdA
 STErrataSheet.pdf\n\nOFFICIAL STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD /
  USE: Finish Ch.16 pp.144–147 + Ch.17 pp.149–153.\nQUIZ / REVIEW: Ch
 .16 quiz p.148.\nWHY: Book provides computer/microprocessor architecture
  while external C resources teach actual programming depth.\nSOURCE RULE
 : The 6th-edition book is the primary CETa textbook. Current ETA compete
 ncy wording + current ETA errata control whenever the older book differs
  or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES
  (WEEK 14)\nBy the end of this event\, you should be able to explain or 
 demonstrate:\n• Explain the build path from C source through compiler/
 linker to an executable/firmware image.\n• Use integer types\, fixed-w
 idth types\, variables\, operators\, conditionals\, loops\, functions\, 
 arrays\, strings\, structs\, and enums.\n• Explain scope\, lifetime\, 
 and basic memory organization.\n• Describe CPU\, RAM\, flash\, buses\,
  peripherals\, registers\, inputs\, and outputs in a microcontroller/com
 puter system.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resources and tasks above 
 are intended to cover every item in this outcome list. If you still cann
 ot explain or demonstrate one of these items after the session\, do not 
 count that item as complete. Write the concept on a short catch-up list 
 and use the linked official or supplemental resource before closing the 
 gap.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned work\, then check th
 e learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or demonstrate an outco
 me\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and repair it durin
 g the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging is required.
