SUMMARY:W15 • Learn — Embedded C + State Machines
DESCRIPTION:ALFRED UNIVERSITY | AU-ESET 301\n\nALFRED STUDY PATH — OPEN
  THESE WHEN YOU NEED THEM\n• Week module: https://numberclaw2.github.i
 o/alfred-university/week.html?week=15\n• Search this topic: https://nu
 mberclaw2.github.io/alfred-university/search.html?q=C%20Programming%20II
 %20%2B%20Embedded%20C%20%2B%20State%20Machines\n• Engineering Library:
  https://numberclaw2.github.io/alfred-university/resources.html\nCAREER 
 CONNECTION: C + MCU bring-up + serial buses + debug instrumentation.\nRE
 SOURCE PRIORITY: CETa Core → Career Core → Deep Dive. Complete the r
 equired work first\; use Deep Dive for repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK 15
  FOCUS: C Programming II + Embedded C + State Machines\nETA/CETa SCOPE: 
 15.5–15.7\; 13.12 reinforcement\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: Pointers\, a
 rrays/strings\, structs/enums\, .c/.h modules\, bitwise masks\, const/vo
 latile\, FSMs\, blocking vs nonblocking design.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOURCES
 :\n- Beej’s Guide to C: https://beej.us/guide/bgc/\n- STM32 MCU Develo
 per Zone: https://www.st.com/content/st_com/en/stm32-mcu-developer-zone.
 html\n\nMASTERY GATE: Write modular C using pointers/structs/enums/bit o
 perations and explain a finite-state-machine design without copying atut
 orial.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: Watching alone never counts. Use learn ->retriev
 e -> calculate/code -> build/measure -> debug -> document. start each no
 n-exam study block with ~10 minutes of spaced recall. Never use a missed
  session as justification for an unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY:\n
 Study only the resources tied to this week’s competencies. Create a on
 e-page summary and 5–10 recall prompts. first 10 min: spaced retrieval
  from older material.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEEP THESE LINKS:\n• Offici
 al Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use your scanned/Google Drive co
 py and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGNMENT.\n• ETA 6th-ed. err
 ata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.pdf\n\nOFFICIAL STUDY GUID
 E — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Ch.17 pp.153–156.\nQUIZ / REVIEW
 : Ch.17 quiz p.156 + Unit 7 review pp.157–158.\nWHY: Book supplies pro
 gramming/control concepts\; Beej/C exercises provide the real code-writi
 ng skill needed for the transition.\nSOURCE RULE: The 6th-edition book i
 s the primary CETa textbook. Current ETA competency wording + current ET
 A errata control whenever the older book differs or omits a current topi
 c.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOMES (WEEK 15)\nBy the end o
 f this event\, you should be able to explain or demonstrate:\n• Use po
 inters\, addresses\, dereferencing\, arrays/pointers\, structs\, and fun
 ction interfaces safely at a practical level.\n• Use bitwise AND/OR/XO
 R\, shifts\, masks\, and set/clear/test operations for hardware-oriented
  code.\n• Explain const\, volatile\, macros\, headers/modules\, and co
 mpiler warnings in embedded C.\n• Distinguish blocking from nonblockin
 g behavior.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resources and tasks above ar
 e intended to cover every item in this outcome list. If you still cannot
  explain or demonstrate one of these items after the session\, do not co
 unt that item as complete. Write the concept on a short catch-up list an
 d use the linked official or supplemental resource before closing the ga
 p.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned work\, then check the 
 learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or demonstrate an outcome
 \, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and repair it during 
 the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging is required.
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Embedded\,CETa
