 %20%2B%20Embedded%20C%20%2B%20State%20Machines\n• Lab Center: https://
 numberclaw2.github.io/alfred-university/labs.html\n• Engineering Libra
 ry: https://numberclaw2.github.io/alfred-university/resources.html\nCARE
 ER CONNECTION: C + MCU bring-up + serial buses + debug instrumentation.\
 nRESOURCE PRIORITY: CETa Core → Career Core → Deep Dive. Complete th
 e required work first\; use Deep Dive for repair or extra depth.\n\nWEEK
  15 FOCUS: C Programming II + Embedded C + State Machines\nETA/CETa SCOP
 E: 15.5–15.7\; 13.12 reinforcement\nCAREER/EMBEDDED PURPOSE: Pointers\
 , arrays/strings\, structs/enums\, .c/.h modules\, bitwise masks\, const
 /volatile\, FSMs\, blocking vs nonblocking design.\n\nTHIS WEEK'S RESOUR
 CES:\n- Beej’s Guide to C: https://beej.us/guide/bgc/\n- STM32 MCU Dev
 eloper Zone: https://www.st.com/content/st_com/en/stm32-mcu-developer-zo
 ne.html\n\nMASTERY GATE: Write modular C using pointers/structs/enums/bi
 t operations and explain a finite-state-machine design without copying a
 tutorial.\n\nSYSTEM RULES: Watching alone never counts. Use learn ->retr
 ieve -> calculate/code -> build/measure -> debug -> document. start each
  non-exam study block with ~10 minutes of spaced recall. Never use a mis
 sed session as justification for an unhealthy make-up marathon.\n\nTODAY
  — ACTION CHECKLIST:\n1. Open LAB-015 — Embedded C State Machine in 
 Practice → Lab Center → Week 15.\n2. Choose one route. Virtual needs
 : Web browser only. Physical needs: C environment or MCU board.\n3. Foll
 ow every numbered step under the route you selected\; predict the result
  before simulating\, building\, or measuring.\n4. Save the required evid
 ence: State diagram + source code + transition log + one error-case expl
 anation.\n5. Mark only the route you actually completed. A virtual compl
 etion does not claim physical bench experience.\n\nREFERENCE HUB — KEE
 P THESE LINKS:\n• Official Associate CET Study Guide\, 6th ed.: use yo
 ur scanned/Google Drive copy and the PRINTED page numbers in BOOK ASSIGN
 MENT.\n• ETA 6th-ed. errata: https://www.etai.org/6thEdASTErrataSheet.
 pdf\n\nOFFICIAL STUDY GUIDE — V6 BOOK ASSIGNMENT\nREAD / USE: Ch.17 pp
 .153–156.\nQUIZ / REVIEW: Ch.17 quiz p.156 + Unit 7 review pp.157–15
 8.\nWHY: Book supplies programming/control concepts\; Beej/C exercises p
 rovide the real code-writing skill needed for the transition.\nSOURCE RU
 LE: The 6th-edition book is the primary CETa textbook. Current ETA compe
 tency wording + current ETA errata control whenever the older book diffe
 rs or omits a current topic.\n\nAFTER TODAY — REQUIRED LEARNING OUTCOM
 ES (WEEK 15)\nBy the end of this event\, you should be able to explain o
 r demonstrate:\n• Use pointers\, addresses\, dereferencing\, arrays/po
 inters\, structs\, and function interfaces safely at a practical level.\
 n• Use bitwise AND/OR/XOR\, shifts\, masks\, and set/clear/test operat
 ions for hardware-oriented code.\n• Explain const\, volatile\, macros\
 , headers/modules\, and compiler warnings in embedded C.\n• Distinguis
 h blocking from nonblocking behavior.\n• Work bit-mask/shift examples 
 and pointer/address reasoning without guessing.\n• Trace state-machine
  transitions and timing/interrupt-related behavior.\n• Design and impl
 ement a finite-state machine for simple embedded behavior.\n• Explain 
 the role of interrupts/timers conceptually and write modular code that i
 s easier to test/debug.\n\nCOVERAGE AUDIT\nThe assigned resources and ta
 sks above are intended to cover every item in this outcome list. If you 
 still cannot explain or demonstrate one of these items after the session
 \, do not count that item as complete. Write the concept on a short catc
 h-up list and use the linked official or supplemental resource before cl
 osing the gap.\n\nWEEK-END MASTERY RULE\nThis practical is the cumulativ
 e proof point for the week. The week is Green only when the knowledge ca
 n be used on real hardware/code/measurements without following a step-by
 -step tutorial.\n\nSIMPLE COMPLETION RULE\nFinish the assigned work\, th
 en check the learning outcomes below. If you cannot explain or demonstra
 te an outcome\, write that one concept on a short catch-up list and repa
 ir it during the next available review block. No spreadsheet logging is 
 required.
